WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.325
Migration ends on a
date; certificates don't.

2
00:00:03.325 --> 00:00:09.187
Public TLS validity falls to
forty-seven days from March 15, 2029.

3
00:00:09.187 --> 00:00:12.517
The schedule binds publicly
trusted certificates,

4
00:00:12.517 --> 00:00:17.626
but the program aligns internal lifetimes
with it as automation proves itself.

5
00:00:17.626 --> 00:00:22.550
The design rule: no certificate class may
depend on a person remembering a date.

6
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.000
The target has three renewal engines.

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.285
Intune profiles renew certificates
on managed devices at the

8
00:00:29.285 --> 00:00:33.075
profile's renewal threshold,
with a new key pair for SCEP.

9
00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:38.084
On Apple platforms, renewal happens only
inside the threshold window with the device

10
00:00:38.084 --> 00:00:40.482
unlocked, and a failed renewal leaves the

11
00:00:40.482 --> 00:00:43.163
expired certificate in
place without a retry.

12
00:00:43.163 --> 00:00:47.587
AD CS autoenrollment renews
domain controller certificates,

13
00:00:47.587 --> 00:00:52.351
which also serve LDAPS, so the CA
must stay online and its CRL current.

14
00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:57.775
And CyberArk Certificate Manager
discovers, tracks and renews server TLS,

15
00:00:57.775 --> 00:01:01.100
including NPS and RADIUS
servers, appliances,

16
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.350
public TLS and network devices, with
a Server Owner on every certificate.

17
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.429
Neither Microsoft issuer
in the target offers ACME,

18
00:01:09.429 --> 00:01:12.938
so the program provides it
through the lifecycle platform.

19
00:01:12.938 --> 00:01:19.313
CyberArk documents the
http-01 and dns-01 challenges.

20
00:01:19.313 --> 00:01:24.107
One federal limit: win-acme requires
FIPS mode to be disabled on the

21
00:01:24.107 --> 00:01:29.425
CyberArk server, so where FIPS mode is
required, use another tested client.

22
00:01:29.425 --> 00:01:34.048
The drift checks run at least weekly and
are committed to the evidence repository,

23
00:01:34.048 --> 00:01:37.175
because an assessor asks how
long a finding stayed open.

24
00:01:37.175 --> 00:01:42.285
Watch for certificates within two renewal
windows of expiry with no engine bound,

25
00:01:42.285 --> 00:01:45.366
issuance outside policy,
missing strong mapping,

26
00:01:45.366 --> 00:01:50.175
trust store anchors that aren't approved,
stale CRLs, and posture regressions.

27
00:01:50.175 --> 00:01:53.068
And treat a SID mismatch
as a security event:

28
00:01:53.068 --> 00:01:56.925
it can mean the certificate
was issued to a different user.

29
00:01:56.925 --> 00:02:01.116
That's event 41 on current
domain controllers,

30
00:02:01.116 --> 00:02:07.580
but event 49 on Server 2008 and 2008
R2, where 41 means no strong mapping.
